sec.t:
In the late 1800s, the fossil hunter Othniel Marsh studied a [[@stegosaurus|Stegosaurus]] skull and found a brain space famously compared to a walnut, in an animal of about 5 tonnes. He also noticed a much bigger space in its hip bones. Newspapers loved the idea of a dinosaur with a second brain in its bottom.

There was no second brain. Birds have a similar space in their hips, filled with nerves and a jelly-like tissue that stores energy. Stegosaurus probably had the same. A small brain was enough for a slow plant-eater that mostly had to find leaves and stay out of trouble.

{{story:crow-smart}}

Hubble doesn't take colour photos the way a phone does. Its [[@cameras-how|cameras]] record light through one coloured filter at a time, as black-and-white images. Scientists then combine them, giving each a colour. Sometimes they include light our eyes can't see, such as [[ultraviolet|ultraviolet]], and show it in a visible colour so we can study it.

{{story:cameras-how}}

The colours are chosen carefully to show real differences, such as which gases are glowing. The famous "Pillars of Creation", towers of gas and dust where new stars are forming, were made this way in 1995.

In October 1959, the Soviet probe Luna 3 swung behind the Moon and took fuzzy photographs. It developed the film on board, scanned it and radioed the images home. People finally saw the hidden side: bright, crater-covered and almost without seas. Soviet scientists named some of its features. That is why the far side has a crater called Tsiolkovsky, after a Russian rocket pioneer, and a dark patch called Mare Moscoviense, the Sea of Moscow.

The first humans to see it with their own eyes were the three Apollo 8 astronauts in December 1968. As they flew behind the Moon, they also lost radio contact with Earth, because the Moon blocked the signal. For about 45 minutes on each orbit, they were cut off from every other person.

{{story:tides}}

HD 189733b isn't the only strange weather report. On WASP-76b, about 640 [[lightyear|light-years]] away, one side always faces its star and reaches over 2,000 °C, hot enough to turn iron into a gas. In 2020, astronomers reported signs that iron vapour drifts to the cooler night side, condenses and may fall as iron rain.

Closer to home, scientists think deep inside Neptune and Uranus, crushing pressure may squeeze carbon into diamonds that sink like hailstones. Lab experiments with powerful lasers have made tiny diamonds from similar chemicals. The details live in the tale of [[@space-weird-diamonds|diamond rain]].

{{story:space-weird-diamonds}}

A dolphin's clicks come from its nose passages and are focused by the rounded, fatty bump on its forehead, called the melon. The clicks bounce off a fish, a rock or a net. The dolphin picks up the echoes through its lower jaw, which passes the sound to its ears.

From the echoes, it can work out how far away something is, how big it is and even what it is made of. [[@bats-fly|Bats]] use the same trick in the air.

{{story:echolocation}}

The passing cloud display works like an animated sign. Dark bands sweep from the back of the cuttlefish's body towards its head, over and over. Scientists have filmed broadclub cuttlefish doing it as they creep up on prey.

One idea is that the moving stripes catch the prey's attention, so it watches the stripes rather than the approaching hunter. Another is that the motion makes the cuttlefish look like drifting shadows, not an animal. Some cuttlefish also use the display to startle prey into moving, which gives away its position.

{{story:octopus-colour}}

The LHC lies about 100 m underground near Geneva. Inside its ring, tiny [[@particle-accelerators|particles]] race in opposite directions at nearly the speed of light and smash into each other. Scientists study the pieces.

To steer the particles, the LHC uses more than 1,200 giant magnets, cooled to about −271°C. That is colder than outer space. In 2012, experiments there discovered a long-predicted particle called the Higgs boson.

{{story:particle-accelerators}}

Chinese records tell a story from 105 CE. A court official named Cai Lun presented the emperor with paper made from tree bark, old rags, hemp and fishing nets. [[archaeologist|Archaeologists]] have found even older paper scraps in China, so he likely improved the method rather than inventing it.

For centuries, the art stayed mostly in East Asia. By the 700s, papermaking had spread to Samarkand in Central Asia, then to Baghdad, where paper mills helped scholars copy books. From the Islamic world it reached Spain and the rest of Europe. Later, the [[@printing-press|printing press]] made huge demands for cheap paper.

{{story:printing-press}}

In 1923, the African American inventor Garrett Morgan patented a new kind of signal. Its extra position stopped traffic in every direction for a moment before letting the other road go. He is said to have been moved by seeing a crash at a busy junction. The pause it gave drivers is similar to the amber light we use today.

Morgan was also known for inventing an early smoke hood, a kind of breathing mask, which he used himself to rescue workers trapped in a tunnel under Lake Erie in 1916.

{{story:algorithms}}

Greek theatres were built into hillsides, with stone seats rising in a half circle around a round floor where the chorus danced. The Theatre of Epidaurus, built in the 300s BCE, seats about 13,000 people.

Its sound is famous. A coin dropped or a match struck on the stage can be heard high up in the back rows. Scientists think the rows of stone seats help, reflecting the voices back towards the audience while soaking up some background noise.

{{story:sound-travel}}

The Swiss mathematician Jacob Bernoulli studied this curve in the late 1600s and fell in love with it. He called it *spira mirabilis*, the wonderful spiral, because it stayed the same however you stretched, turned or flipped it.

He asked for one to be carved on his gravestone with the Latin words "Eadem mutata resurgo", meaning "Though changed, I rise again the same". The carver, perhaps not a mathematician, cut an Archimedean spiral instead, where the gaps stay equal rather than growing. You can still see the mistake on his memorial in Basel Minster.

{{story:fibonacci-nature}}

A [[@prime-numbers|prime number]] is a whole number with exactly two factors: 1 and itself. So 2, 3, 5 and 7 are prime. The number 1 has only one factor, itself, so it isn't prime.

This wasn't always agreed. For centuries many mathematicians did list 1 as a prime. Eventually most dropped it because of a beautiful rule: every whole number above 1 can be built by multiplying primes in exactly one way. 12 is 2 × 2 × 3, and nothing else. If 1 were prime, you could add as many 1s as you liked, and "exactly one way" would stop being true.

{{story:computers-how}}

When the modern [[@olympics|Olympics]] began in Athens in 1896, a French scholar, Michel Bréal, suggested a race from Marathon to Athens to honour the legend. It was about 40 km long.

A Greek water carrier named Spyridon Louis won it, to the joy of the home crowd. No Greek had yet won a running or field event at those Games, so his victory made him a national hero.

{{story:ancient-greece}}

The best-known image generators use a method called diffusion. During training, the computer takes real pictures and adds random speckles to them, a little at a time, until nothing is left but noise. Then it practises the reverse: removing a little noise at each step so the picture comes back.

Once it is good at that, you can hand it pure static and a sentence. Step by step, it removes the "noise" in a way that matches your words. After a few dozen steps, a cat in a spacesuit appears out of nothing. Underneath, the whole thing is just a grid of [[@pixels|pixels]], each one given a colour by a lot of arithmetic.

{{story:art-forgery}}

Every connection between neurons has a number called a weight. At the start, the weights are random, so the network's guesses are rubbish. Then training begins. The network sees a photo labelled "cat", makes a guess and checks how wrong it was.

A method called backpropagation then nudges every weight a tiny bit in the direction that would have made the guess better. Do this with thousands or millions of labelled photos and the weights slowly settle into values that work. It is a bit like tuning thousands of radio knobs at once until the music comes through clearly.

{{story:learning-how}}

The Company built Fort St George in Madras, now [[@chennai|Chennai]], in the 1640s. Bombay, now Mumbai, came to the English Crown in 1661 as part of a royal wedding gift from Portugal, and the Crown rented it to the Company for £10 a year.

In 1690, the Company set up a post on the Hooghly river that grew into Calcutta, now [[@kolkata|Kolkata]]. Each of these forts grew into a great city, and today they are among India's largest.

{{story:words-borrowed}}

Fast food made sense because of how Romans lived. In big cities, poorer families rented rooms in tall blocks of flats called *insulae*. Some were five or six storeys high, taller than most school buildings today, and built partly of wood.

Cooking on an open fire in a wooden flat was a good way to burn the whole block down. So many flats had no kitchen. Families bought hot food outside, the way many Mumbai office workers grab vada pav at a station stall. Rich Romans, who had big houses with kitchens and cooks, often sneered at snack bars as rough places for the poor.

{{story:street-food}}

The second Guru, Guru Angad, helped shape the Gurmukhi script, used to write the Gurus' hymns and Punjabi today. The third, Guru Amar Das, expanded langar and asked that everyone who came to see him eat there first. Sikh tradition says even the emperor [[@akbar|Akbar]] sat on the floor to eat.

The fourth Guru, Guru Ram Das, founded the town that became [[@amritsar|Amritsar]]. The fifth, Guru Arjan, built the Harmandir Sahib there, with four doors to welcome people from every direction. In 1604 he compiled the Adi Granth, the holy book, which includes hymns by the Gurus and by saints such as [[@mirabai-kabir|Kabir]], Namdev and Sheikh Farid. In 1606 he was executed under the emperor Jahangir, and Sikhs remember him as their first martyr Guru.

{{story:punjabi-gurmukhi}}

The temples collected and handed out huge amounts of grain, oil, wool and animals. Officials needed to keep track. At first they used small clay tokens, then drew pictures on clay tablets. Over time the pictures became patterns of wedge-shaped marks pressed with a cut reed. "Cuneiform" means "wedge-shaped" in Latin.

Thousands of these tablets survive. Many are school exercises written by children learning to be scribes, complete with mistakes. Some are copies of the *Epic of Gilgamesh*, the story of a king of Uruk who searches for the secret of living forever. It's one of the oldest great stories in the world.

{{story:writing-began}}

In the 1800s, a big lake called the Haarlemmermeer kept growing in storms and eating up land near Amsterdam. Between 1849 and 1852, steam engines pumped it dry.

On that old lake bed now sits Schiphol, [[@amsterdam|Amsterdam's]] main airport. Its runways lie about 3 m below sea level. In fact, "Schiphol" is often said to mean "ship hole", because ships were wrecked in storms on the old lake.

{{story:sea-level-rise}}

In Gokul, Krishna is a handful. He and his friends sneak into homes to steal butter and curd hung from the ceiling in pots. When the pots are too high, the boys climb on each other's shoulders. In Maharashtra today, teams build human towers to break a pot of curd during the dahi handi festival, copying this tale.

One day, Yashoda hears he has eaten mud. She asks him to open his mouth. Inside, she sees the sun, the moon, the stars and the whole universe. Then she forgets what she saw, and he is just her little boy again. Many people see this as the heart of the stories: a mighty god who chooses to be a child.

{{story:art-cheriyal}}

The Portuguese captured Goa in 1510 and ruled it until 1961, when it became part of India. They came for the spice trade, travelling the sea [[@trade-routes|trade routes]] around Africa.

{{story:goa-liberation}}

Portuguese ships carried plants from their colony in Brazil and other parts of the Americas. Chillies, potatoes, tomatoes, pineapples and cashews all reached India through ports like Goa. Within a century or two, chillies had spread across the whole [[subcontinent|subcontinent]].
